<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Korea and Japan</p><h1 class="st-title">Azai clan domain</h1><p class="st-meta">1523 CE – 1573 CE (approx.) · capital Odani</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The domain of the Azai of northern Omi, whose lord Azai Nagamasa betrayed his brother-in-law Oda Nobunaga and was destroyed in 1573.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Azai were retainers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyogoku-clan">Kyogoku</a> governors of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/omi-province">Omi</a>. In the 1520s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azai-sukemasa">Azai Sukemasa</a> took power from his masters in a period of internal strife and built the mountain fortress of Odani, overlooking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-biwa">Lake Biwa's</a> northern shore.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The domain lay on the main roads between the capital and eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azai-sukemasa">Sukemasa</a>'s son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azai-hisamasa">Hisamasa</a> was overshadowed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rokkaku-clan">Rokkaku</a> of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/omi-province">Omi</a>, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azai-nagamasa">Nagamasa</a> defeated them in 1560 and married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oichi">Oichi</a>, sister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oda-nobunaga">Oda Nobunaga</a>, around 1567, an alliance that secured Nobunaga's route to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1570 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azai-nagamasa">Nagamasa</a> honoured an older alliance with the Asakura and attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oda-nobunaga">Nobunaga</a>. Defeated at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-anegawa">Anegawa</a> that year, he held out until 1573, when Odani fell and he killed himself. His daughters, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yodo-dono">Chacha</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ohatsu">Hatsu</a> and Go, later shaped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-clan">Toyotomi</a> and Tokugawa politics.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/azai-clan-domain.jpg" alt="Azai clan domain" width="320" height="320"><figcaption>Image: Wikiwikiyarou · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Japanese_Crest_mitumori_Kikkou_ni_Hanabishi.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1523 CE – 1573 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Odani</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Azai Sukemasa, Azai Hisamasa, Azai Nagamasa</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Middle Japanese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Azai clan, Asai clan</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1548&amp;focus=azai-clan-domain" aria-label="Azai clan domain on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="8125 896 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Azai clan domain lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M8445 1152L8448 1163"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8448 1163h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8445 1152h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8445 1152h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Odani. <a href="/atlas?year=1548&amp;focus=azai-clan-domain">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

